[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Elasticsearch Index Lifecycle Management (ILM) and Rollover Tutorial"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Managing time-series data like logs, metrics, and audit trails in Elasticsearch requires a scalable strategy. As data grows, keeping indices at an optimal size and deleting old data automatically prevents performance degradation and disk exhaustion. Index Lifecycle Management (ILM) automates this process through distinct phases: Hot, Warm, Cold, and Delete."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the ILM Phases and Rollover"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The rollover action is the cornerstone of effective time-series management. Instead of letting a single index grow indefinitely, rollover creates a new index once the current index reaches a specific age, document count, or maximum size. This prevents shards from becoming too large, which can negatively impact search and indexing performance."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Create an ILM Policy"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, define an ILM policy that dictates when an index transitions between phases and when it rolls over. In this example, the policy rolls over indices when they reach 50GB or 30 days of age, and deletes them after 90 days."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"PUT _ilm/policy/logs_rollover_policy
{
  "policy": {
    "phases": {
      "hot": {
        "actions": {
          "rollover": {
            "max_size": "50GB",
            "max_age": "30d"
          }
        }
      },
      "delete": {
        "min_age": "90d",
        "actions": {
          "delete": {}
        }
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Create an Index Template with ILM Integration"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To apply the ILM policy automatically to new indices, configure a component template and an index template. Notice the use of the 'logs-' write index alias, which is mandatory for rollover-enabled data streams or indices."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"PUT _index_template/logs_template
{
  "index_patterns": ["logs-*"],
  "template": {
    "settings": {
      "number_of_shards": 1,
      "number_of_replicas": 1,
      "index.lifecycle.name": "logs_rollover_policy",
      "index.lifecycle.rollover_alias": "logs-write"
    }
  }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Bootstrap the Initial Index"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"When using rollover with ILM, you cannot simply start writing to an alias directly. You must create an initial bootstrap index that acts as the first write index, with a suffix matching a numeric pattern (like -000001)."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"PUT logs-000001
{
  "aliases": {
    "logs-write": {
      "is_write_index": true
    }
  }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 4: Verify the ILM Status"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can inspect the execution state of your managed indices by querying the ILM explain API. This will reveal the current phase, action, and any potential allocation or rollover errors."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"GET logs-000001/_ilm/explain"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for ILM and Rollover"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep primary shard sizes between 10GB and 50GB for search-heavy workloads."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use index templates and write aliases instead of hardcoding index names in your ingestion pipelines."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Monitor ILM execution regularly using the explain API to detect stuck phases early."}]
